{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass on the day that Moses finished.</b> כָּלַת is written — on the day the Mishkan was erected, Israel was like a bride entering the bridal canopy (Pesikta; Tanḥuma)." "<b>When Moses finished.</b> Betzalel and Oholiav and every wise-hearted man made the Mishkan, and Scripture attributes it to Moses, because he devoted himself to it — to see the form of each and every thing as He had shown him on the mountain, to instruct the craftsmen, and he erred in no form. And so we find with David: because he devoted himself to the building of the Temple, as it says, ״זְכוֹר ה' לְדָוִד אֵת כָּל עֻנּוֹתוֹ אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּע לַה'״ and so forth (Psalms 132), therefore it is called by his name, as it says (I Kings 12) ״רְאֵה בֵיתְךָ דָּוִד״ (Tanḥuma)." "<b>When Moses finished erecting it.</b> And it does not say 'on the day he erected' — this teaches that all seven days of the inauguration Moses would set it up and take it apart, and on that day he set it up and did not take it apart; therefore it says ״כַּלּוֹת מֹשֶׁה לְהָקִים״ — that day concluded his erections. It was Rosh Chodesh Nisan; on the second [of Nisan] the heifer was burned, on the third the first sprinkling was performed, and on the seventh they shaved (Sifrei)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:09:49.012068360Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}